Usage: {[command]s} <protocol xmls>... [options] -o <output directory>

OPTIONS
  -o, --output <directory>

    The directory to generate protocol.zig files into.

    Each protocol.xml file specified will have it's interfaces generated into a
    zig file with a matching name. For example, `wayland.xml` would become
    `wayland.zig`.

    In addition, a file importing all of the generated protocols named `root.zig`
    will be created. You should use this file as the `root_source_file` of
    module.

  --compat-output <file>

    The path to generate a libwayland compatible protocol.zig file into.

  --interface-version <interface name> <version>

    Target a specific version of an interface.

  --import <protocol.xml> <import>

    Specify the path to import an already generated protocol xml.

    This is directly used to generate import statements, and will look
    something like the following:

        @import("zig_module").path.to.protocol

    Wayland XML files are not namespaced, so the only way to know where an
    interface comes from is to check each protocol XML.

TLDR

  Generate zig protocol definitions from xml protocol definitions:

      {[command]s} protocol1.xml protocol2.xml ... -o path/to/output_directory/

  Limit an interface to a specific version:

      {[command]s} protocol.xml --interface-version interface version -o path/to/output_directory/

  Import an already generated protocol:

      {[command]s} protocol.xml --import path/to/previous_protocol.xml @import("zig_module").and.path -o path/to/output_directory/


